Subject: Diagnostic: rollback inputs, replay and outgoing deterministic data
Upstream-base: 9046e67aa2077a7eb4ed6e28c9df3c1d3b368f84

Rollback study. Under RECHARGED_ROLLBACK_TEST the port's rollback (in the
game module) saves the match after every fixed step and goes back a few
steps to run them again. A replayed step must get exactly the inputs the
first run got, so the routers report what they apply before each step
(every machine's packed player inputs and the deterministic data events
they queue for the simulation, in their order), and ApplyRollbackInputs
applies a report again. FixedUpdateTask passes the port a replay step:
those inputs and the simulation, no pads, no capture, nothing sent.

The rollback restores the drawing's objects with the match, so the port
replays whole frames around the steps: their tasks run again as they did,
each with the time step it had (the task manager reports every task's,
mscharged_rollback_task_time), so that the drawing recomputes its state
with the match's. A replay leaves out the pads, sound, network, disc and
movies: BeginFrameTask serves no disc reads, FrontEndTask reads no pads
and neither resets nor opens the HOME Button Menu. FixedUpdateTask's part
is its work before the steps (the time scale's transition) and the
recorded steps (RollbackReplayFixedFrame); EndFrameTask's part ends the
frame without sending it (RollbackReplayEndFrame): the views emptied and
the frame's memory used afresh in the same half
(mscharged_rollback_reuse_frame_memory), as the other may still be read
for the frame sent before. The port goes back at the start of a frame,
before any task of it runs (nlTaskManager::RunAllTasks).

Drawing has nowhere to go during a replay, whose frame was already drawn:
GLView drops any model or packet attached meanwhile.

Deterministic data the simulation queues to send (player visibility, mega
strike results, NIS loaded) is an input of a later step on every machine:
a replayed step queues none, its data went out when it first ran. While it
waits in the router's queue it lives outside the game's arenas, which a
rollback restores while the router's queue goes on.

The mega strike meter's presses go from the shooter's machine to the others
outside the inputs, in their own messages, which cGame queues for the
shooter's fielder on the other machines. The port records the presses
received before each step and gives them again to its replay
(RollbackRedeliverMegaStrikeMeter); a replayed step sends none, nor the
mega ball pointer of a captain's shot. Nor does it start rumbles: the pads
felt them when the steps first ran, and the rumbles' state stays out of the
rollback with the pads, which PlatPadUpdateTask drives every frame.

The rollback leaves the frame memory alone: it is used afresh every frame,
with its cursors in glxMemory's statics. mscharged_rollback_frame_memory
returns pointers to its blocks.

Network matches with rollback (RECHARGED_ROLLBACK): the network router lets
the port decide whether a step can run (HasInput) and apply its inputs
(OnInputReady), so that a step whose inputs have not arrived from another
machine runs with that machine's last inputs, and goes back when they
arrive and differ. The router's queue limit is the port's, fixed for the
match on every machine; each step's inputs leave as soon as they are
captured (one tick per packet); a step undone and run again does not capture
and send its inputs a second time (NetworkInputManager::CaptureInputs),
which also leaves a capture out, or sends the same inputs again, when the
port shortens or lengthens this machine's input delay by a step (the other
machines apply its messages one per step, in order); and
the sync data an input message carries is the checksum of the last step
whose inputs had all arrived, which the port compares with the other
machines' (the state now may rest on predicted inputs); every other message
carries instead the frame word alone (flag 2, which the original never
sets), with the port's measure for its time sync.

The audio plays on through rollbacks: its state lives in a heap of its own,
which no rollback restores. The functions the game calls into the audio
(the 45 the rest of the module uses, the audio system's creation in main,
and the callbacks the audio hands the AX, alarms, the pads, disc reads and
its bundles' loading)
enter and leave it, and inside it AllocateMemory takes the port's
audio allocator. A replayed frame starts, stops, pauses and resumes no
sound: it did when it first ran. The audio's streams read the disc while
rollbacks happen: mscharged_rollback_async_read_state returns the disc
reads' manager, which the port leaves out of the rollback.

Without RECHARGED_ROLLBACK_TEST or RECHARGED_ROLLBACK nothing changes.

